course: biology
lesson: 377
title: Ukraine's plants: sunflowers, viburnum, poppies, lime trees and steppe grasses
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In July a Ukrainian field turns into a yellow sea of sunflowers taller than a grown-up. Each big head is not one flower but hundreds or over a thousand tiny flowers packed together, and later each becomes a seed.
explain: Young sunflowers turn to follow the sun across the sky, then swing back east at night. Once they bloom most stop and face east, where the morning sun warms them and bees visit more.
explain: Some plants become symbols because people see them every day near home: kalyna with red winter berries, sung about in Oi u luzi chervona kalyna, and the red poppy with a black centre for remembering the dead of the Second World War.
explain: Under it all lies chornozem, black earth, among the richest soils in the world, built over thousands of years by steppe grass roots rotting into the ground. Feather grass, wild tulips and lime trees each have clever tricks to live here.
fact: Each sunflower head is hundreds or over a thousand tiny flowers, and its seeds sit in spirals that, counted one way and the other, often give 34 and 55, numbers from a famous number pattern.
fact: Ukraine is one of the world's biggest sunflower growers and sells more sunflower oil than any other country.
fact: Young sunflowers follow the sun from east to west by day and swing back east at night; open flowers mostly face east, warm faster in the morning and get more bee visits, scientists found.
fact: Sunflowers came from North America, reached Europe in the 1500s, and oil was pressed from them in the Russian Empire in the 1800s; historians report it spread partly because it was allowed during church fasts when butter was not.
fact: Kalyna, guelder rose or viburnum, is a symbol of Ukraine; its berries are very bitter until frosts soften them, birds eat them in winter, and each berry has one flat seed.
fact: Oi u luzi chervona kalyna is a song of the Ukrainian Sich Riflemen from 1914 that became famous again around the world in 2022, and it says kalyna, Ukraine, will rise again.
fact: Ukraine uses a red poppy with a black centre to remember the dead of the Second World War.
fact: Poppies grow in disturbed soil, so they come up on fields and roadsides after digging, and the seed head shakes out seeds through a ring of little holes like a pepper pot.
fact: Lime trees, lypa, bloom in early summer, bees make light lime honey from them, and lime flower tea is a traditional drink for colds.
fact: Each bunch of lime flowers hangs from a long pale leaf-wing that later helps the seeds spin away like a helicopter.
fact: Feather grass, kovyl, has seeds with long feathery tails that twist when dry and untwist when damp, slowly screwing the seed into the soil; its sharp seeds can stick in dogs' ears, so check pets after walks.
fact: Ukraine's black earth, chornozem, was built over thousands of years by steppe grass roots dying and rotting into the ground, and in places it is about a metre deep.
fact: Schrenk's tulip grows wild in southern Ukraine and is a wild relative of garden tulips; it flowers early in spring before grass grows tall and summer dries everything, then hides as a bulb.
exercise: What is a big sunflower head really made of? | Hundreds or over a thousand tiny flowers | One giant yellow petal folded round and round | A single big seed | Leaves that turned yellow in the sun
exercise: Which country sells more sunflower oil than any other? | Ukraine | Iceland | Egypt | Japan
exercise: How do young sunflowers move? | They follow the sun east to west by day and swing back at night | They never move at all, from the day they sprout | They turn away from the sun to keep cool | They spin round and round all night like a wheel
exercise: Which way do most open sunflowers face? | East | West | Straight down | They keep turning all summer
exercise: Why do bees visit east-facing sunflowers more? | They warm up faster in the morning | They smell much more strongly of honey | They have more petals than the others | They are taller than all the others around
exercise: Where did sunflowers first come from? | North America | Ukraine, on the steppe | Northern China | Australia, from the dry centre
exercise: Why did sunflower oil spread in the Russian Empire, historians report? | It was allowed during church fasts when butter was not | It was cheaper than water | Kings ordered everyone to eat it | It was the only oil that would not freeze in winter
exercise: Why does kalyna keep its berries until winter? | Frost softens their bitterness, and birds eat them then | The berries are glued on to the branches by a sticky juice | Nobody likes them at all | They turn into leaves in spring
exercise: How many seeds are in one kalyna berry? | One flat seed | About a hundred tiny seeds | No seeds at all | Exactly ten seeds
exercise: What is Oi u luzi chervona kalyna? | A 1914 song of the Sich Riflemen, famous again in 2022 | A recipe for berry jam | A new pop song written in 2022 for a talent show | The name of a Kyiv street
exercise: What does Ukraine's red poppy with a black centre stand for? | Remembering the dead of the Second World War | Good luck at the start of the school year | The start of the harvest | A happy birthday
exercise: Why do poppies come up on fields after digging? | They grow well in disturbed soil | Digging plants poppy seeds | Poppies only grow in rain | Diggers drop red paint
exercise: How do lime tree seeds travel away? | A pale leaf-wing spins them like a helicopter | Birds carry them in their beaks to new places | They float down rivers | They roll like balls
exercise: What do bees make from lime tree flowers? | Light lime honey | Beeswax candles only | Sour green lemon juice | Nothing, bees avoid them
exercise: How does a feather grass seed get into the ground? | Its tail twists and untwists, screwing the seed into the soil | A mole pushes it in | It falls straight down like a stone | Rain washes it into cracks that worms leave behind
exercise: How was chornozem made? | Steppe grass roots dying and rotting over thousands of years | Volcanoes pouring thick black ash over the whole steppe long ago | Coal dust from mines | Farmers painting the soil
exercise: Why does Schrenk's tulip flower so early in spring? | Before grass grows tall and summer dries everything | Because it cannot stand the cold snow on its leaves | Because bees only fly in winter | Because it dies in May
test: Count them: a poppy has 4 red petals. How many petals on 6 poppies? | 24 | 10 | 20 | 30
test: Count them: a wild tulip has 6 petal parts. You see 3 tulips. How many petal parts? | 18 | 9 | 12 | 24
test: Count them: sunflower seed spirals counted one way give 34 and the other way 55. How many more spirals one way? | 21 | 89 | 19 | 11
test: In Plants vs. Zombies sunflowers make sunshine for the garden. What is right about real sunflowers? | They use sunlight to make food; they do not make sunshine | They glow at night | They make light for other plants | They store sunshine in their seeds to shine in winter
test: «sunflowers have always been ukrainian, like forever» What is right? | They came from North America and reached Europe in the 1500s | They first grew wild in the steppe near Kyiv | They came from the Moon | They came from Africa only last year on a big cargo ship to Odesa
test: «mate's dog keeps shaking its head after a steppe walk» What might be the cause? | A sharp feather grass seed stuck in its ear | The dog is dancing to music | The dog likes the wind | The dog is cold and wet after swimming in a pond
test: How can a plant from another continent become a national symbol? | People grow and see it every day for hundreds of years | It must be planted by a king | It has to be invented in that country by its own scientists | It changes its colour to match the flag
test: «i'll eat the kalyna berries off the bush now, it's summer» What will they taste like? | Very bitter, until frosts soften them | Sweet like strawberries and cream | Salty like crisps | Like nothing at all, just like water from a tap
test: Why would grass roots make richer soil than a pine forest's needles? | Many roots die and rot deep in the ground every year | Pine needles are made of metal | Grass makes soil out of air and rain all by itself, without any help | Pine trees drink all the soil
test: What would happen to poppies on a field that is never dug or ploughed again? | Other plants would grow over it and the poppies would slowly fade out | There would be many more poppies every single year, until the field was all red | Poppies would turn into trees | Poppies would become blue
test: Why is a self-drilling seed useful on hard dry steppe ground? | It plants itself where it could not just fall in | It makes the soil softer for cows | It helps the seed fly far away | It keeps birds from flying
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a sunflower head ought not to be regarded as a single flower. | It consists of hundreds or upwards of a thousand minute flowers | It is a leaf that has turned yellow | It is a fruit rather than a flower, since it contains a great many seeds | It is composed of a single enormous petal
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the sunflower. | It originated in North America and reached Europe in the sixteenth century | It originated in Ukraine | It was first cultivated in China | It was introduced from Australia in recent decades by merchants and travellers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of chornozem? | It was formed over millennia through the decay of the roots of steppe grasses | It was deposited by volcanic eruptions | It consists of coal dust | It was formed within a single century by the labour of agriculture and fertilisers
exam: Ascertain the means by which the seed of feather grass is conveyed into the soil. | Its awn twists when dry and untwists when damp, driving the seed into the soil | It is buried by moles | It falls vertically under its own weight | It is washed deep into the ground by the flood water of the spring rivers each year
